  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how intestinal fungi interact with bacteria, metabolites, and host immunity in the colon and small intestine, and how these interactions can either support barrier health or promote inflammation. Across both regions, fungal overgrowth or altered fungal communities appear alongside changes in bacterial populations, including reduced beneficial anaerobes and expansion of pathobionts. In the colon, fungal products and tryptophan-derived metabolites are shown influencing epithelial cells and immune pathways such as AhR, IL-17, IL-22, and RORγt, which shape barrier protection and ILC3/TH17 responses. Other panels highlight bile acid and short-chain fatty acid pathways, suggesting that microbial metabolites can dampen or promote inflammation through T regulatory cells, dendritic cells, and cytokine signaling.\n\nThe figure also depicts several mechanisms of mucosal damage: fungal invasion, epithelial DNA damage, inflammasome-related signaling, and toxins such as candidalysin. These are linked to recruitment of neutrophils and activation of fungus-specific cytotoxic T cells. In the small intestine, Peyer’s patches, M cells, IgA, and Paneth cell antimicrobial peptides are emphasized, indicating immune sampling and control of fungi near the crypts and villi. Overall, the image presents intestinal fungal dysbiosis as tightly connected to microbiome imbalance, metabolite shifts, barrier dysfunction, and chronic gut inflammation.",
